This gives an arbitrary-rank certificate at a fiber-volume weighted lattice centerpoint. Its exact loss is the total log-concave deficit over the directed primitive rays in a base halfspace. In particular, if at most \(R\) immediately descending directed rays can occur in any such halfspace, the certificate has the exact coefficient
\[
\frac{\gamma_d\chi_d}
{\gamma_d+(R-1)(\gamma_d-\chi_d)}
\]
for \(R\geq1\), while \(R=0\) has coefficient \(\chi_d\). The theorem is uniform in the continuous dimension and gives a dimension-independent bound whenever \(R\) is bounded. It does not settle the locked problem because \(R\) can depend on the integer rank, and it does not state the already accepted \(n=1\) theorem as a new result.
